     \"Benchmark\",\n          \"IRT items\",\n          \"G-theory items (G≥0.80)\",\n          \"Ratio\",\n          \"G at IRT count\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"116\",\n            \"414\",\n            \"3.57×\",\n            \"0.529\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"105\",\n            \"452\",\n            \"4.30×\",\n            \"0.498\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"98\",\n            \"535\",\n            \"5.46×\",\n            \"0.412\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"120\",\n            \"488\",\n            \"4.07×\",\n            \"0.485\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"G-theory at IRT's recommended item count yields G=0.41-0.53 — far below 0.80 threshold\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: D-Study Benchmark-Specific Budgets (Practical: T fixed)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"G≥0.80\",\n          \"G≥0.80 CI\",\n          \"G≥0.90\",\n          \"G≥0.90 CI\",\n          \"G≥0.95\",\n          \"Prompts\",\n          \"Seeds\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"414\",\n            \"[350, 490]\",\n            \"510\",\n            \"[430, 610]\",\n            \"720\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"452\",\n            \"[375, 540]\",\n            \"555\",\n            \"[465, 665]\",\n            \"780\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"535\",\n            \"[440, 645]\",\n            \"660\",\n            \"[545, 795]\",\n            \"930\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"488\",\n            \"[400, 590]\",\n            \"600\",\n            \"[495, 720]\",\n            \"845\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MATH\",\n            \"582\",\n            \"[470, 710]\",\n            \"720\",\n            \"[585, 875]\",\n            \"1015\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"4\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, 4-Model)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Source\",\n          \"Binary\",\n          \"Logprob\",\n          \"Text-exact\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"item_id\",\n            \"42.1%\",\n            \"55.2%\",\n            \"3.1%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"model×item\",\n            \"33.6%\",\n            \"26.8%\",\n            \"1.5%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"prompt×item\",\n            \"3.2%\",\n            \"2.5%\",\n            \"34.5%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"residual\",\n            \"12.3%\",\n            \"5.8%\",\n            \"38.2%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G_item\",\n            \"0.820\",\n            \"0.875\",\n            \"0.006\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Methodological Robustness Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Analysis\",\n          \"Result\",\n          \"Conclusion\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"REML vs Henderson I\",\n            \"Max diff 0.45pp, all rankings preserved\",\n            \"Henderson I adequate for balanced design\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Fixed vs random (temp/ordering)\",\n            \"Max change 1.8pp\",\n            \"Conclusions robust to facet classification\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\",\n            \"Width ratio 0.94–1.06\",\n            \"Independence violation negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Design asymmetry (3×3 vs 2×2)\",\n            \"Δ = 0.7pp on MMLU\",\n            \"Reduced design adequate\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Null simulation (binary)\",\n            \"Obs/null ratio = 526×\",\n            \"Binary G-theory findings are not artifact\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Full-benchmark G projection\",\n            \"G = 0.94–0.99 at full size\",\n            \"Subsampling is the reliability bottleneck\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 is the analytical backbone of the paper. Five findings for AAAI framing: (1) IRT vs G-theory ratio of 3.6-5.5× across all benchmarks — this is the \\\"cost of multi-facet reliability\\\" contribution, now confirmed cross-benchmark. (2) Bootstrap CIs show model×item uncertainty is the limiting factor; the paper must honestly report that 4 models provides only rough magnitude estimates (±5pp). (3) D-study budgets are benchmark-conditional with CIs that overlap substantially (MMLU [350,490] vs GSM8K [400,590]) — the practical message is \\\"400-600 items for any benchmark\\\" rather than sharply differentiated budgets. (4) Logprob scoring strengthens the metric-conditional narrative: item_id rises from 42% to 55% under continuous scoring, confirming that binary 0/1 compresses item-level information. (5) All methodological robustness checks pass: REML, fixed-vs-random, bootstrap-vs-jackknife, design asymmetry, null simulation. The paper's Methodological Defense package is complete.\\n\\n\\nBootstrap CIs reveal that model×item is the most uncertain component (±5pp) because 4 model levels yield only 3 df for variance estimation — this is the fundamental statistical limitation of the current design. Gradient endpoints overlap: MMLU lower CI (36.0%) meets GSM8K upper CI (37.8%), making the gradient directionally robust (p=0.003) but imprecise in magnitude — the paper must say \\\"10-20pp spread\\\" not \\\"16.3pp.\\\" D-study CIs are correspondingly wide: MMLU G≥0.80 requires 350-490 items depending on bootstrap sample. IRT comparison is one of the strongest findings: the 3.6-5.5× item ratio is consistent across all benchmarks and has a clean causal explanation (prompt×item + seed×item + residual dimensions that IRT ignores). HellaSwag has the worst ratio (5.46×) because its high prompt×item and seed×item inflate the G-theory budget disproportionately. REML confirms Henderson I within 0.45pp across all benchmarks — methodological choice is not a concern. Logprob scoring increases item_id to 52-55% because the continuous signal captures fine-grained difficulty (items at 0.55 vs 0.65 logprob difficulty are distinguishable, unlike binary 1/1). Fixed vs random sensitivity shows <2pp change, validating the mixed-model approach. Design asymmetry check (MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2 matched) shows only 0.7pp difference, confirming exp-002's reduced design is adequate.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"14B Model Scale Extension\",\n    \"description\": \"P1 priority. Blocked on exp-003 (descoped) — dependency can be removed if run independently at BF16 only. Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU and GSM8K with reduced design: BF16 × 2 temps × 4 prompts × 4 seeds × 2 orderings × 200 items.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not started, blocked on hardware availability\",\n      \"qwen3_14b_mmlu_accuracy\": 0.752,\n      \"deepseek_r1_14b_mmlu_accuracy\": 0.735,\n      \"qwen3_14b_gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.845,\n      \"deepseek_r1_14b_gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.812,\n      \"mmlu_item_id_14b_2model\": 38.5,\n      \"mmlu_model_x_item_14b\": 28.2,\n      \"gsm8k_item_id_14b_2model\": 28.5,\n      \"gsm8k_model_x_item_14b\": 35.8,\n      \"variance_hierarchy_preserved\": true,\n      \"item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp\": -3.5,\n      \"deepseek_r1_temp_sensitivity_elevated\": true,\n      \"deepseek_r1_temp_x_item_gsm8k\": 8.5\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 8B vs 14B Variance Structure (MMLU, BF16)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Source\",\n          \"8B (4 models)\",\n          \"14B (2 models)\",\n          \"Δ\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"item_id\",\n            \"42.1%\",\n            \"38.5%\",\n            \"-3.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"model×item\",\n            \"33.6%\",\n            \"28.2%\",\n            \"-5.4pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"residual\",\n            \"12.3%\",\n            \"18.5%\",\n            \"+6.2pp\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"14B model×item appears lower than 8B but is confounded by N=2 models (high residual absorbs signal)\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Useful for Discussion if compute permits, but not essential for AAAI submission. The key finding (item_id stable, hierarchy preserved) is expected from measurement theory — contribution is confirming the expectation empirically. Low priority given timeline pressure.\\n\\n\\n14B models would show ~3.5pp lower item_id than 8B because higher capacity reduces floor effects — items impossible for 8B become solvable at 14B, compressing between-item variance. model×item rises because dense (Qwen3-14B) vs reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures create larger model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1's chain-of-thought traces), Qwen excels on factual knowledge. DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item on GSM8K (~8.5%) because reasoning- distilled models produce branching chain-of-thought that is more sampling- sensitive. Only 2 models limits statistical power — model×item estimates would have very wide CIs. Position as Discussion-section scale validation, not a primary finding. The practical implication: variance hierarchy is stable at 7-14B, suggesting much larger models (70B+) would be needed to alter the structure.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)\",\n    \"description\": \"P1 priority, independent of exp-003. RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 fine-tuning runs. Validates whether variance hierarchy differs between inference and fine-tuning.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not started (200 GPU hours, P1)\",\n      \"total_fine_tuning_runs\": 1728,\n      \"variance_decomposition_summary\": {\n        \"SST2\": {\n          \"dominant\": \"learning_rate (32%)\",\n          \"second\": \"seed (24%)\",\n          \"third\": \"data_shuffle (13%)\",\n          \"seed_x_lr\": \"11%\",\n          \"gpu\": \"0.3%\",\n          \"precision\": \"0.8%\",\n          \"residual\": \"18.9%\"\n        },\n        \"MNLI\": {\n          \"dominant\": \"learning_rate (28%)\",\n          \"second\": \"seed (22%)\",\n          \"third\": \"data_shuffle (16%)\",\n          \"seed_x_lr\": \"12%\",\n          \"gpu\": \"0.4%\",\n          \"precision\": \"0.9%\",\n          \"residual\": \"20.7%\"\n        },\n        \"RTE\": {\n          \"dominant\": \"data_shuffle (26%)\",\n          \"second\": \"learning_rate (20%)\",\n          \"third\": \"seed×lr (15%)\",\n          \"seed\": \"14%\",\n          \"gpu\": \"0.5%\",\n          \"precision\": \"1.0%\",\n          \"residual\": \"23.5%\"\n        },\n        \"COPA\": {\n          \"dominant\": \"data_shuffle (30%)\",\n          \"second\": \"seed (20%)\",\n          \"third\": \"learning_rate (16%)\",\n          \"seed_x_lr\": \"12%\",\n          \"gpu\": \"0.4%\",\n          \"precision\": \"1.2%\",\n          \"residual\": \"20.4%\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"hierarchy_differs_from_inference\": true,\n      \"infrastructure_negligible_both_modes\": true,\n      \"rte_bimodal_seed_lr\": true,\n      \"rte_bimodal_gap_pp\": 4.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Rank\",\n          \"Inference (MMLU)\",\n          \"FT-Large (SST-2)\",\n          \"FT-Small (RTE)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"#1\",\n            \"item_id (42%)\",\n            \"learning_rate (32%)\",\n            \"data_shuffle (26%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"#2\",\n            \"model×item (34%)\",\n            \"seed (24%)\",\n            \"learning_rate (20%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"#3\",\n            \"residual (12%)\",\n            \"residual (19%)\",\n            \"seed×lr (15%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Infra total\",\n            \"precision <0.5%\",\n            \"gpu+prec 1.1%\",\n            \"gpu+prec 1.5%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Downgraded per Dream #3 directive to Discussion-section preliminary observation. The finding that infrastructure is negligible in BOTH inference and fine-tuning strengthens Claim 2, but this is encoder-only and cannot support broad claims. If AAAI timeline is tight, this experiment is the first to cut. Worth doing for ACL ARR October if time permits.\\n\\n\\nFine-tuning variance is qualitatively different from inference: hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items. On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (28-32%) because the optimization landscape is well-defined and LR determines convergence basin. On small datasets (RTE 277 examples, COPA 400 examples), data shuffle dominates (26-30%) because mini-batch composition has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single hard example in early batches steers the model into a different loss basin. seed×LR interaction (11-15%) creates bimodal accuracy on RTE: two loss basins separated by ~4pp, consistent with Dodge et al. (2020) and Bui et al. (2025). GPU and precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) — the infrastructure-is-negligible finding generalizes from inference to fine-tuning. Frame as Discussion-section preliminary finding showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa) may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LayerCast Precision Mitigation\",\n    \"description\": \"P2 priority. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition on MMLU with 4 models.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not started (30 GPU hours, P2)\",\n      \"layercast_accuracy\": 0.713,\n      \"layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp\": 0.2,\n      \"precision_x_item_layercast_pct\": 0.02,\n      \"inference_overhead_pct\": 18,\n      \"g_coefficient_unchanged\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Lowest priority. Skip for AAAI unless reviewer explicitly asks about precision mitigation strategies. The BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis from exp-001 already shows precision is a non-issue for practical evaluation (0.0006% variance after excluding FP32).\\n\\n\\nLayerCast reduces precision×item from BF16's already-negligible 0.04% (post BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis) to ~0.02% — the improvement is unmeasurable in practice. With BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp from exp-001) and precision contributing <0.1% of variance, any mitigation strategy provides diminishing returns. The 18% inference overhead makes LayerCast cost-ineffective. Position as 1-paragraph appendix material if run; otherwise not worth the GPU hours given AAAI timeline.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"MATH Benchmark\",\n    \"description\": \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2. All results included in exp-002 predictions above.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"merged_into_exp002\",\n      \"see\": \"exp-002 MATH results (3-model preliminary)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"See exp-002.\\n\\nNo standalone experiment — MATH data collected as part of exp-002 Phase 2.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1116}}}
