{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011xTGDmYXr4YobtkiV8rdQZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Research Patterns\n3\tdescription: Experimental consensus on variance sources, decomposition methods, and gaps in LLM evaluation reproducibility (10 papers, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: research\n5\tsource_papers: 10\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Variance Source Taxonomy\n9\t\n10\t**Infrastructure variance** (4/10; high confidence):\n11\t- Numerical precision: BF16→9% accuracy std; FP32→~0% (Yuan 2025). Reasoning models most affected.\n12\t- Batch-size dependence: 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention (ThinkingMachines 2025).\n13\t- Background randomness at T=0: GPT~0.075, Gemini~0.065, Claude~0 effective temperature (Messina TMLR 2026).\n14\t\n15\t**Methodological variance** (6/10; high confidence):\n16\t- Judge choice dominates safety eval (43.8% variance); prompt~50% in open models, ~25% commercial (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026).\n17\t- Much apparent prompt sensitivity is evaluation artifact — LLM-judge raises rank correlation 0.30→0.95 (Hua EMNLP 2025).\n18\t- Standard CIs omit 40-60% uncertainty; naive 95% CIs→79% coverage at scale (Mesting 2026).\n19\t\n20\t## Decomposition Frameworks\n21\t\n22\t| Framework | Paper | Method |\n23\t|-----------|-------|--------|\n24\t| TEE (G-theory) | Mesting 2026 | REML + D-study, facets: item/prompt/judge/temp |\n25\t| Total Variance | Brittlebench 2026 | V_data + V_brittleness via ANOVA |\n26\t| Intent Decomp | Kunievsky 2025 | intent/articulation/model uncertainty |\n27\t| Background Temp | Messina 2026 | Reference calibration→single T_bg scalar |\n28\t\n29\tG-theory adaptation is the most rigorous approach (Mesting). 4/10 papers use variance decomposition. **High confidence.**\n30\t\n31\t## Benchmarks & Models\n32\t\n33\tTop benchmarks: MMLU (3), GPQA (3), MATH (3), ARC (2), TruthfulQA (2). Models: Llama/Qwen/GPT families (5 papers each); 7B-8B open-weight as standard testbed. 10-100 runs per config is norm. **High confidence.**\n34\t\n35\t## Mitigations\n36\t\n37\t- LayerCast (BF16 store+FP32 compute): <3.4% divergence, 34% memory savings (Yuan). **Medium.**\n38\t- Batch-invariant kernels: determinism at 1.6-2.1× latency (ThinkingMachines). **Medium.**\n39\t- TEE-guided design: halves RMSE; 3 judges→38% SE reduction (Mesting). **Medium.**\n40\t\n41\t## Critical Gap — Our Niche\n42\t\n43\t**No paper unifies infrastructure (precision/batch/hardware) with methodological (prompt/judge/temp) variance in one G-theory framework.** Mesting applies G-theory to methodological facets only; Yuan/ThinkingMachines characterize infra variance without G-theory. Additional gaps: no cross-lingual analysis; training-time seed variance disconnected from inference-time; no standardized variance reporting protocol. **Support: 10/10 (absence), high confidence.**\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20f76664-8d08-43f7-a19b-5ac1c53e3195","uuid":"900806d1-b871-45b8-8f7a-f8f47cacbc68","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:18:20.663Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-research.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Research Patterns\ndescription: Experimental consensus on variance sources, decomposition methods, and gaps in LLM evaluation reproducibility (10 papers, 2024-2026)\ntype: research\nsource_papers: 10\n---\n\n## Variance Source Taxonomy\n\n**Infrastructure variance** (4/10; high confidence):\n- Numerical precision: BF16→9% accuracy std; FP32→~0% (Yuan 2025). Reasoning models most affected.\n- Batch-size dependence: 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention (ThinkingMachines 2025).\n- Background randomness at T=0: GPT~0.075, Gemini~0.065, Claude~0 effective temperature (Messina TMLR 2026).\n\n**Methodological variance** (6/10; high confidence):\n- Judge choice dominates safety eval (43.8% variance); prompt~50% in open models, ~25% commercial (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026).\n- Much apparent prompt sensitivity is evaluation artifact — LLM-judge raises rank correlation 0.30→0.95 (Hua EMNLP 2025).\n- Standard CIs omit 40-60% uncertainty; naive 95% CIs→79% coverage at scale (Mesting 2026).\n\n## Decomposition Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Paper | Method |\n|-----------|-------|--------|\n| TEE (G-theory) | Mesting 2026 | REML + D-study, facets: item/prompt/judge/temp |\n| Total Variance | Brittlebench 2026 | V_data + V_brittleness via ANOVA |\n| Intent Decomp | Kunievsky 2025 | intent/articulation/model uncertainty |\n| Background Temp | Messina 2026 | Reference calibration→single T_bg scalar |\n\nG-theory adaptation is the most rigorous approach (Mesting). 4/10 papers use variance decomposition. **High confidence.**\n\n## Benchmarks & Models\n\nTop benchmarks: MMLU (3), GPQA (3), MATH (3), ARC (2), TruthfulQA (2). Models: Llama/Qwen/GPT families (5 papers each); 7B-8B open-weight as standard testbed. 10-100 runs per config is norm. **High confidence.**\n\n## Mitigations\n\n- LayerCast (BF16 store+FP32 compute): <3.4% divergence, 34% memory savings (Yuan). **Medium.**\n- Batch-invariant kernels: determinism at 1.6-2.1× latency (ThinkingMachines). **Medium.**\n- TEE-guided design: halves RMSE; 3 judges→38% SE reduction (Mesting). **Medium.**\n\n## Critical Gap — Our Niche\n\n**No paper unifies infrastructure (precision/batch/hardware) with methodological (prompt/judge/temp) variance in one G-theory framework.** Mesting applies G-theory to methodological facets only; Yuan/ThinkingMachines characterize infra variance without G-theory. Additional gaps: no cross-lingual analysis; training-time seed variance disconnected from inference-time; no standardized variance reporting protocol. **Support: 10/10 (absence), high confidence.**\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ku49acKrjng5fcze6ycrgc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\n3\tdescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: writing\n5\tsource_papers: 8\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Story Types\n9\t\n10\t- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n11\t- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n12\t- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n13\t- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n14\t\n15\t## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n16\t\n17\t\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n18\t\n19\t## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n20\t\n21\t(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n22\t\n23\t## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n24\t\n25\t(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n26\t\n27\t## Figure Conventions\n28\t\n29\t**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n30\t\n31\t**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n32\t\n33\t**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n34\t\n35\t## Table Conventions\n36\t\n37\t**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n38\t\n39\t**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n40\t\n41\t**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n42\t\n43\t**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n44\t\n45\t## Our Paper Blueprint\n46\t\n47\tOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20f76664-8d08-43f7-a19b-5ac1c53e3195","uuid":"a728ef87-83f1-4239-a1f2-f85f5f6e5170","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:18:20.665Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-writing.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\ndescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\ntype: writing\nsource_papers: 8\n---\n\n## Story Types\n\n- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n\n## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n\n\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n\n## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n\n(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n\n## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n\n(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n## Figure Conventions\n\n**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n\n**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n\n**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Table Conventions\n\n**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n\n**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n\n**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Our Paper Blueprint\n\nOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SLZiZLVABhRnN7yhNeSjgD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me write the full LaTeX paper. I need to ensure the output directory exists first.\n\nLet me create a comprehensive paper with:\n\n1. Abstract\n2. Introduction (4 paragraphs + contributions)\n3. Related Work\n4. Method (G-theory background, experimental design)\n5. Experiments - sections for each experiment:\n   - 5.1 Multi-Benchmark G-Study (exp-002) - main results\n   - 5.2 Comprehensive Combined Analysis (exp-004) - robustness & analysis\n   - 5.3 Multi-Hardware G-Study (exp-003) - descoped, future work\n   - 5.4 14B Model Scale Extension (exp-005) - projected\n   - 5.5 Fine-Tuning G-Study (exp-006) - projected\n   - 5.6 LayerCast Precision Mitigation - projected\n   - 5.7 MATH (merged into exp-002)\n6. Discussion\n7. Conclusion\n\nThe MATH benchmark is merged into exp-002 so I'll note that in that section. For descoped and planned experiments, I'll add subsections with tables showing projected results clearly marked. I need to reframe the gradient findings from monotonic to benchmark-conditional and work through the key claims carefully.\n\nFor the claims, I'm softening the confound analysis, restricting MMLU to precision-only results, and strengthening claim 3 with benchmark-specific budgets. I'll structure the experiments section to include all seven experiments in the JSON, with MATH as a subsection within exp-002 results, and descoped/planned experiments as subsections with clearly labeled projected results.\n\nI'm settling on a traditional paper structure: introduction, related work, method, then results organized around the cross-benchmark variance analysis as the main contribution, with extended analyses for the 14B model, fine-tuning, and LayerCast as planned work. Each experiment will have its own section or subsection with corresponding tables, starting with the cross-benchmark variance decomposition as the core result. I'm realizing there are too many separate sections here—I need to consolidate the experiments into subsections under Results instead. So the main structure becomes Results with six subsections: the cross-benchmark G-Study as the primary analysis, combined analysis and robustness checks, hardware crossing as descoped, scale extension to 14B models, fine-tuning variance structure, and precision mitigation with LayerCast. MATH gets merged into the first subsection rather than standing alone. Now I'm ready to start writing the LaTeX.\n\nI need to keep the key empirical findings front and center: the 1.27M records from exp-002 across multiple models and benchmarks, the cross-model variance ranging from 25.8-42.1% on item_id and 32.8-38.5% on model×item interactions, the format confound showing multiple-choice at 40% versus completion and free-form both around 29%, the D-study results indicating 414-582 items needed for G≥0.80, the out-of-sample validation showing minimal drift, and the comparison showing G-theory captures 3.6-5.5× more variance than IRT alone.\n\nFor the scoring analysis, I'm noting that logprob scoring substantially increases the item_id variance component from 42.1% to 55.2%, and I'll include bootstrap confidence intervals across all variance components. The planned experiments—exp-005, exp-006, and LayerCast—have projected results in the data that I should present clearly as projected rather than confirmed findings. I'm being careful to only use numbers from the actual experiment_data.json, avoid any narrative guidance in the paper itself, and explicitly acknowledge the non-monotonic gradient issue and format confound as limitations. The infrastructure crossing work should be framed as confirmatory validation of the measurement model. I've settled on presenting the completed experiments as main results and the planned ones with their projected data as preliminary analyses, since this is a dream paper simulation. Now I'm drafting the LaTeX document to structure the paper with proper formatting, packages for tables and citations, and a clear title about reproducibility budgets and generalizability theory.\n\nI'm reorganizing the sections to consolidate the experiments more logically—grouping the cross-benchmark variance analysis with the MATH results, combining robustness checks, and nesting the hardware and scale experiments under an extended experiments section to avoid too many top-level sections.\n\nFor a conference paper with typical page constraints, I'm tightening the structure further by merging background and related work, then consolidating all experiments under a single section with subsections for each study, which should fit better within the 7-8 page limit while keeping the narrative flow clear.\n\nWriting the LaTeX document...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2918,"cache_read_input_tokens":48261,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2918},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20f76664-8d08-43f7-a19b-5ac1c53e3195","uuid":"a16c5795-24a4-4587-bf58-790dbb786d5c"}
